  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Mobile Optimization**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the `Header.tsx` file to pinpoint mobile optimization bottlenecks. Line 27\'s classnames, specifically the font sizing and layout, seem like a prime suspect for further investigation. I\'ll be looking into how these style attributes render on smaller screens and if they cause any layout shifts or visual clutter.\n\n\n**Evaluating Mobile Ergonomics**\n\nI\'ve moved on from `Header.tsx` to assess the overall mobile experience. The current design in `Header.tsx`, with its fixed width and adjacent buttons, overflows on smaller screens. I\'m focusing on navigation, as there\'s no bottom bar, which, according to my research, is key for thumb reach. The wizard\'s header padding, specifically the `p-6` on mobile in `QuoteWizard.tsx`, is also taking up too much space.\n\n\n**Refining Mobile UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on specific UI components within the wizard and beneficiary sections. Preset buttons in `QuoteWizard.tsx`, specifically those on line 224, are wrapping awkwardly on smaller screens, and segmented tabs, line 254, may clip due to their current design, requiring a visual review. I\'ve also found an over-generous padding on the wizard body in `QuoteWizard.tsx` line 307. Lastly, I\'m examining beneficiary and copay cards layout, focusing on responsive design.\n\n\n**Improving Mobile Navigation**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the mobile navigation within the quote wizard, and the lack of bottom navigation buttons is a real problem. Adding \"Next\" and \"Back\" buttons at the bottom of each step would vastly improve the mobile user experience. In contrast to the quote wizard header, the quote wizard footer is great in comparison, but I\'ll make sure it\'s optimized for mobile!\n\n\n**Adjusting Mobile Layouts**\n\nI\'m now focused on the UI elements\' layouts, particularly on the GPS button and \"Mudar UF\" state buttons tray. The GPS button\'s full width is good, so I need to make sure the tray of buttons can scroll horizontally. I think a `<select>` or a compact multi-column grid would be much cleaner.\n\nI\'m also reviewing plan cards in `PlanCard.tsx`, where I\'ve identified several areas for refinement. The card\'s padding, especially on smaller screens, can be reduced to prevent content cramping. Additionally, I need to evaluate the price display and savings layout on small mobile screens to prevent text from wrapping awkwardly, and the button layout at the bottom.\n\n\n**Optimizing Actionable Elements**\n\nMy current focus is optimizing mobile interactions within the comparison results. I need to ensure the \"Receber Proposta\" button offers a minimum 44px tap target or full width on smaller screens, and that the comparison button\'s tap target is comfortably reachable. The tier filter buttons in the results summary bar could look broken on mobile if they wrap, so I\'m investigating how to keep these buttons in a scrollable view.\n\n\n**Enhancing Mobile Comparisons**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `SideBySideModal` component, which presents a challenging mobile user experience due to its tabular format. The core of my efforts lies in addressing the issue of the first column disappearing on horizontal scroll, leading to a loss of context. I\'m exploring solutions such as a sticky first column, and the potential for a toggle between a side-by-side table with a sticky first column and a card-based view to improve mobile usability. I\'m also ensuring the touch targets within the modal are appropriately sized.\n\n\n"
